<section class="report-page research-page" id="{{ market }}-geography"><span class="eyebrow">{{ market }} / Geography and concentration</span><h2>Where the advertised market is concentrated.</h2><p>The two largest observed locations account for {{ study.top_two_share }}% of {{ market }} listing-weeks in this period. Source location labels include cities and state-level markets. This describes the platform's geographic exposure; it is not a share of national housing stock or transactions. Concentrated coverage means national summaries are especially sensitive to the product mix in these cities.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Source location</th><th>Average weekly active</th><th>Share of listing-weeks</th><th>Areas</th><th>First discovered</th><th>Reduction events</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for city in study.cities %}<tr><td>{{ city.city|location }}</td><td>{{ '{:,.1f}'.format(city.average_active) }}</td><td>{{ city.share|share }}</td><td>{{ city.areas }}</td><td>{{ city.new }}</td><td>{{ city.reductions }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table><p class="running-title">Source: PS-0 source CSV, restricted to the included market weeks. Average inventory uses the report's {{ study.report_weeks }} week starts. Shares use summed active listing-weeks ({{ '{:,}'.format(study.total_listing_weeks) }}), not deduplicated properties.</p><h3>What this means for comparisons</h3><p>Use city and class benchmarks before interpreting an aggregate asking price. A large supply of land in one city and flats in another can change the national mix without any comparable property becoming more expensive. New discoveries also reflect collection activity, and should not be treated as construction completions.</p><h3>What to monitor next</h3><p>Compare geographic and property-class shares in the next complete issue before attributing a change in headline asking prices to market movement. Check whether collection coverage or the mix of areas has changed. For development or policy decisions, combine this advertised inventory with independently verified planning, pipeline and household evidence.</p></section>
<section class="report-page research-page"><span class="eyebrow">{{ market }} / City briefs</span><h2>Read each submarket on its own terms.</h2>{% for city in study.briefs %}<h3>{{ city.city|location }}</h3><p>{{ city.city|location }} averages {{ '{:,.1f}'.format(city.average_active) }} active listings per included week across {{ city.areas }} areas. {{ city.leading_class }} is the largest observed class, contributing {{ city.leading_class_share }}% of the city's listing-weeks. The source records {{ city.new }} first discoveries and {{ city.reductions }} reduction events.</p><table><thead><tr><th>High-activity area / class</th><th>Asking-price benchmark{{ ' / yr' if rent else '' }}</th><th>Average active / observed week</th><th>Priced / report weeks</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for row in city.benchmarks[:4] %}<tr><td>{{ row.neighbourhood }}<br><small>{{ row.property_class }}</small></td><td>{{ row.price|money }}</td><td>{{ row.active }}</td><td>{{ row.priced_weeks }} / {{ study.report_weeks }}</td></tr>{% else %}<tr><td colspan="4">No area/class groups meet the inventory and usable-price criteria.</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table><p class="running-title">Selected by observed inventory, not price. Benchmarks are means of group medians. Broad classes can still mix different bedroom counts, sizes and conditions.</p>{% endfor %}<h3>Practical reading</h3><p>Buyers and tenants can use these observations to identify locations for a more detailed specification-matched search. Valuers should validate individual comparable properties and achieved prices. Developers should check whether competing advertisements match their proposed product; observed concentration alone does not demonstrate unmet demand.</p></section>
{% for pair in study.price_books|batch(2) %}<section class="report-page research-page price-book"><span class="eyebrow">{{ market }} / Area price book</span><h2>Broader comparisons within property classes.</h2>{% if loop.first %}<p class="running-title">For each of the four most active classes, show up to ten eligible area groups selected by average observed inventory, then ordered by city and benchmark price. This includes middle-priced areas, but is an activity-selected sample rather than a complete or representative price distribution. At least three average weekly active listings and a usable price are required.</p>{% endif %}{% for book in pair %}<h3>{{ book.name }}</h3><p class="running-title">Within this selected sample, {{ book.lowest.neighbourhood }} ({{ book.lowest.city|location }}) has the lowest benchmark at {{ book.lowest.price|money }}; {{ book.highest.neighbourhood }} ({{ book.highest.city|location }}) has the highest at {{ book.highest.price|money }}{{ " / yr" if rent else "" }}. These differences also reflect property mix.</p><table><thead><tr><th>City / area</th><th>Mean group median{{ ' / yr' if rent else '' }}</th><th>Average active</th><th>Priced / report weeks</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for row in book.rows %}<tr><td>{{ row.city|location }} / {{ row.neighbourhood }}</td><td>{{ row.price|money }}</td><td>{{ row.active }}</td><td>{{ row.priced_weeks }} / {{ study.report_weeks }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>{% endfor %}<p class="running-title">Interpret broad class matches with care. Bedrooms, floor area, condition and tenure may differ within a class. Land plot size and per-plot/per-unit quoting are not normalised. These are advertised benchmarks, not property valuations. Full eligible observations: {{ evidence_link("comparisons.csv", "aggregate comparison table") }}.</p></section>{% endfor %}
